[00:06] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'So, this is what a typical permanent outdoor lights installation looks like, and there\'s no sugar coating it. During the day, this looks bad. It looks tacky, and it\'s something that the majority of people will never install on their homes if this is what the end results look like. Now, I couldn\'t be more excited'

[00:18] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'for this video because I was able to get Jacob, or as you might know him as JRo Inc. from his YouTube channel, who\'s the number one GOI permanent outdoor lights installer in the entire country and creator of the Perm Track to show us just how easy it is to do a perfect'

[00:30] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'install, including splices, jumps, and Ysplits that\'s wife approved, HOA approved, and something that\'s pretty much invisible during the day and spectacular at night. Now, make sure to stay till the very end for all the final results, some commentary, plus a little'

[00:43] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'friendly bet on who\'s the best disc golfer between the two of us. So, the first thing Jacob and I worked on was putting together a plan. You want to figure out where the power will be and then if needed map out where it makes sense for jumps and splits to occur. For'

[00:55] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'my house, what we thought was best was to put the start right here and work my way around towards the front. Then once we got to this point, we\'re going to make the jump up to the second level. We\'ll work our way going right. And once we get to this point, we\'ll be doing a'

[01:07] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Ysplit so that our lights on the top continue along the path, but simultaneously things will jump back down to the bottom level and finish up at the end of the garage. So, this is the perm track that Jacob created in response to the majority of consumers that don\'t like ugly wires'

[01:20] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'hanging all over their house. It\'s designed specifically to fit the GOI permanent pro kit and gives people like you and me the ability to create a professional install at the fraction of the price that lighting companies charge. And if you\'ve ever reached out to some of these installation companies'

[01:33] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'to check pricing, you know exactly what I\'m talking about. Here I have a strand of the pro lights, which is currently GOI\'s best model. Take the bottom track piece and you can place the puck on top of the pad and then line up another track next to it. And part of the beauty'

[01:45] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'of this product is how simple it actually is. Snap on both covers to completely conceal the cords. And then snap the puck light cover on and you\'re done. And whether you use screws or a brad nailer, the bottom of the track has a'

[01:57] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'small groove to make sure it\'s centered. And if you are using screws, it helps hold things in place. And for this install, we ended up using these sheet metal tech screws, which worked perfect on my vinyl sophets. A few other things that you\'re going to be doing a lot of'

[02:09] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'is making corners. Instead of trying to cut two tracks at a 45 degree angle with the miter saw, the kit comes with 90° angle connectors. So, all you have to do is line up two pieces and snap the connector in place to make a perfect turn. And of course, when you\'re at the'

[02:21] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'end of a run, you get a couple end caps for a clean finish. Now, if you already have white sophets, you\'re in luck. But for everyone else, painting these to match is something I highly recommend doing, which we\'ll be going over later on in the install. Jacob uses these tape'

[02:33] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'circles to cover up the lights to protect them from the paint. and then removes them when done. And as always, I\'ll leave links to everything in the description that we\'re using in this project to make it easy for you to replicate. We\'ll also be going over how to install this recess power/controller'

[02:46] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'housing towards the end of the project in case that\'s something you\'re interested in. So, we\'re going to tackle the upper level first, starting in this back corner and working our way out towards the front. And throughout this install, I\'ll do a mix of close-up'

[02:58] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'footage and time-lapses so you can get a nice variety of shots. Now, everyone might have a different opinion, but Jake thinks that four inches out from the house is in fact the sweet spot. So, he always cuts a 4-in section out from the track and put some sticky backing on it to use as a guide throughout the entire'

[03:11] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'install. This is actually a really important part to get the same consistent lighting effect. So, definitely make sure to use a guide since your eyes can sometimes play tricks on you. As we come to our first corner, this is a great chance to better understand the thought process from'

[03:24] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'someone who does this for a living. &gt;&gt; The light doesn\'t reach to get over here on the face. because we don\'t want it really right on the corner because it\'s going to the it kills the uh continuity.'

[03:36] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '&gt;&gt; Mhm. &gt;&gt; You know, you can have a light like here and then this one can be over here cuz you\'re changing faces of the home. So, you\'re not going to ever notice that like to the naked eye like'

[03:48] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'architecturally. But when one comes up a little short like this, what we what we really need to do is is extend it a little bit so we can get it here and get our full dome. Gotcha. So, this brings us to the first splice of the day, which'

[04:02] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Jacob is more than comfortable doing on the ladder and has all the tools needed to do it on the fly. But to make it easier to follow and understand, let\'s go over a close-up of what he\'s doing. In this example that he\'s faced with using the normal length of the GOI'

[04:14] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'strand, our puck light is too close to the corner, and we want to be able to extend that light further out. What you want to do first is cut the GOI lights in the middle of the strand that you\'re wanting to extend. Next, you can strip back the outer black layer on each side, which reveals three strands. The power,'

[04:27] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'data, and ground. We\'ll need to strip back all of those and then give them a good twist. We can then take our big roll of'

[04:41] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'extension cable that you can buy on Amazon and cut off the length that we need to add to the run. Strip back the outer sheath where you\'ll see three individual wires. Strip those back and again twist. Now we can connect the cut goi wire back'

[04:53] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'together by splicing in our added length. This makes it super easy to stay consistent because you can go red to red, yellow to yellow, and then that just leaves blue to black. Once we have them all twisted to the correct color on'

[05:05] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the one side, these gel filled connectors are perfect for securing the wires together to help keep out the moisture. Once the wires are in, press down not too hard using a crimping tool. And then give a tug to make sure it\'s in there good. Then do this for the other two wires. Once that side\'s done, do the'

[05:18] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'exact same thing with the other cut strand of the GOI lights and use the same connectors to secure that side in place. Now we can begin to put things back. But I\'ll be measuring a new track to take into account the distance that we added.'

[05:31] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'And these are made of rigid PVC and are very difficult to cut with a regular kitchen scissors. So make sure to pick up something like this that\'ll make cutting super easy. And these tracks were specifically designed with DIYers in mind, giving you plenty of space to'

[05:43] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'hide the splice connectors in the channels regardless of the method you use. So, if you wanted to use wire nuts or even wiggle connectors instead of the blue crimps that I\'m using, those would also fit in the track and still be able to close. And just like that, we were able to'

[05:56] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'extend the length. Here\'s the before and here\'s the after. Now, on the flip side, if you\'re wanting to shorten the length, there\'s no need to cut any wires to do that. Simply cut the track down to the size you want and then fold the cord into whatever length'

[06:08] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'and then snap the tracks back in place. And another intentional design is for all the clips to overlap the tracks. This means you don\'t have to be perfect with your cuts since the putt covers, corner connectors, and end caps will hide gaps caused by you making a cut a'

[06:20] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'little too short or not straight. So, going back to the install, everything that I just showed you can absolutely be done on the fly and up on the ladder like Jake\'s doing right now. And in case you\'re wondering, we\'re'

[06:33] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'keeping the old strands up there for now, which we\'ll pull down at the end. Now, one thing that a lot of you guys are going to have to factor in that I don\'t have to with my house is peaks. For this, you can just cut the tracks at an angle to somewhat get it close to what you have. And then use a putt cover'

[06:46] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'at the high point of the peak like you\'re seeing here. Then just make sure that the light before and immediately after are positioned at equal distances from the peak for that perfect symmetrical look. From there, it was just a few more corners with a couple more splices to make sure that they were'

[06:58] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'exactly two lights on each brick wall and that they were both centered at the same spot so you\'d get the full cone effect. So, as he\'s finishing up the top section, if you remember back to the beginning, I mentioned that we\'d be doing one Y-split where the lights would'

[07:10] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'continue their run on top, but at the same time, there\'d be another strand of lights that would start back up on the bottom level and work their way to the end of the garage. To better show you how this is done, let\'s go back to our previous corner example, and I\'ll use this to demonstrate.'

[07:22] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'I\'ll first cut off the male end connector on an extra string of lights I have. Strip back the outer sheath, and then strip back the three wires just like we did previously. Now, even though I\'m using this connector, you could also'

[07:34] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'use the extra black splice wire that we used to extend the corner. So, don\'t think you have to do it this way. Then, I\'ll be cutting off the blue connectors from one of the splices that we made and re-repping those wires'

[07:46] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'again. Next, we\'ll want to combine all three red wires together, all three yellow wires, as well as the blue and black wires.'

[07:58] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Once that\'s complete, we can then crimp on some new blue connectors to keep everything together. So, I\'ll begin to put things back'

[08:11] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'together, including cutting a small notch out of the track so we can get this connector exposed. Now, because I\'m using an end connector,'

[08:28] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'I could easily attach a new string of lights like I\'m doing here. Or you could use one of Go\'s extension cables if you need to jump a further distance like I have to do in my install. And to explain what a Ysplit or what some call a T-split is doing is at this point right'

[08:41] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'here, the data is now continuing along its normal path up and around, but the exact same data signal is now being sent in this new direction. So, this is exactly what we\'re doing now. and Jake had a white 12-oot extension cable left over from a different project that\'ll'

[08:54] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'hide out of sight behind the wood trim as we make the jump from up top to the bottom at the Ysplice junction. And I will make sure to leave a link to the clips that he\'s using to get these to stay in place. So thankfully the most timeconuming part'

[09:07] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'is now done and all that\'s left to do is to finish up the lower sections. And to make the jump from down here to the top part, we used a couple GOI extensions combined with some more splicing cable from Amazon that you\'ve already seen a couple times now. Then the lower level'

[09:19] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'by the garages was the last part that needed to be finished before moving on. It\'s now time for the best part, which is painting. And color matching is what makes this entire setup become pretty much invisible during the day. You can certainly hand paint this, but a sprayer'

[09:31] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'is for sure the way to go. These smaller ones do have incredible accuracy in a small spray zone so you can get super precise. As far as strategy, if you haven\'t done something like this before, definitely use some cardboard to help protect the house. You also pretty much'

[09:44] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'only have to get the front and underneath. Don\'t worry about getting the back except for around the corners since that\'s the only time you would see the backside. This is also the time that you would'

[09:57] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'remove the yellow tape over the lights once you\'ve painted over them. This was for sure my favorite part to watch, seeing how everything was now starting to come together with the end nearly in sight. Now, as he continues to'

[10:10] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'paint, I did want to mention that if you do want full frontto back coverage of lights on your house, but the 200 foot go kit would be too small to go all around, you\'re going to want to buy two separate GOI kits and install one on the front portion of your house like we\'re'

[10:22] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'doing on mine, and then the second kit would be installed in the back area. After all the painting was done, it was time for power. Ironically, I just did a review of the new Yuthi NVR security system, and I ran PoE wires in my house from the outside. And to do that, I'

[10:35] [scene:talk]
